<quiz>
<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Absolute and percentage error of an estimate</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>Nyambura used the trapezoidal rule with \({@ns@}\) strips to estimate \(\displaystyle \int_{ {@lo@} }^{ {@hi@} } \left({@fx@}\right)dx\) and got \({@exd(Tv)@}\).</p><p>(a) Find the exact value of the integral.</p><div>Exact value \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div><p>(b) Find the absolute error of her estimate.</p><div>Absolute error \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div><p>(c) Find the percentage error, correct to 2 decimal places.</p><div>Percentage error \(=\) [[input:ans3]] % [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>]]></text>
  </questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html">
    <text><![CDATA[<p>The absolute error is the size of the difference between the estimate and the exact value. The percentage error compares it with the exact value: absolute error \(\div\) exact value \(\times 100\).</p><p>(a) \(\displaystyle \int_{ {@lo@} }^{ {@hi@} } \left({@fx@}\right)dx = \left[{@Fx@}\right]_{ {@lo@} }^{ {@hi@} } = {@exd(Ex)@}\).</p><p>(b) Absolute error \(= |{@exd(Tv)@} - {@exd(Ex)@}| = {@exd(ae)@}\).</p><p>(c) Percentage error \(= \dfrac{ {@exd(ae)@} }{ {@exd(Ex)@} } \times 100 \approx {@dispdp(float(floor(10^2*(pc)+1/2)/10^2),2)@}\,\%\).</p><p>[[if test="is(Tv>Ex)"]]The estimate is too big: the curve bends upwards, so the chords lie above it.[[else]]The estimate is too small: the curve bends downwards, so the chords lie below it.[[/if]]</p>]]></text>
  </generalfeedback>
  <defaultgrade>3</defaultgrade>
  <penalty>0.1</penalty>
  <hidden>0</hidden>
  <idnumber></idnumber>
  <stackversion><text>2026063000</text></stackversion>
  <questionvariables>
    <text><![CDATA[/* exd: shortest exact decimal display of a terminating value (up to 3 d.p.) */
exd(v) := if integerp(v) then v else if integerp(10*v) then dispdp(float(v),1) else if integerp(100*v) then dispdp(float(v),2) else dispdp(float(v),3);
trap(fn, a, b, m) := block([h, ys], h: (b-a)/m, ys: makelist(subst(x=a+j*h, fn), j, 0, m), h/2*(ys[1]+ys[m+1]+2*sum(ys[j], j, 2, m)));
/* Design: [c0, c1, c2, c3, lo, hi, n]; odd entries bend upwards, even entries bend downwards. Source: original. */
L: [[1,0,0,1,0,2,4],[30,2,-3,0,1,3,2],[2,2,0,2,0,3,3],[30,2,0,-1,0,3,3],[1,2,6,1,1,4,3],[30,0,0,-1,0,3,3],[5,2,6,0,0,2,4],[40,0,-3,-1,0,2,2],[2,0,3,1,1,5,4],[20,2,0,-1,0,2,2]];
ix: 1+rand(10);
cf: L[ix];
lo: cf[5]; hi: cf[6]; ns: cf[7];
fx: cf[1]+cf[2]*x+cf[3]*x^2+cf[4]*x^3;
Fx: integrate(fx,x);
Tv: trap(fx, lo, hi, ns);
Ex: subst(x=hi,Fx)-subst(x=lo,Fx);
ae: abs(Tv-Ex);
pc: ae/Ex*100;
/* diagnostics */
pcT: ae/Tv*100;
pcno: ae/Ex;
/* test values */
pc2: float(floor(10^2*(pc)+1/2)/10^2);
pcT2: float(floor(10^2*(pcT)+1/2)/10^2);
ftb: abs(Tv-Ex-2);
ftc: float(floor(10^2*(abs(Tv-Ex-2)/(Ex+2)*100)+1/2)/10^2);]]></text>
  </questionvariables>
